5
00:00:10.279 --> 00:00:11.175
Problem.

6
00:00:12.425 --> 00:00:19.294
Generate unique, roughly time-ordered 64-bit ids on many machines with no coordination per id.

7
00:00:19.574 --> 00:00:26.870
Handle 4,096 plus ids in the same millisecond, and the clock moving backwards (an NTP step).

8
00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:29.095
Part 2.

9
00:00:29.375 --> 00:00:30.292
Key idea.

10
00:00:31.542 --> 00:00:45.601
Pack 41 bits of milliseconds since a custom epoch (about 69 years), 10 bits of machine id (1,024 nodes) and 12 bits of sequence (4,096 per ms per node) into one long.

11
00:00:45.881 --> 00:00:53.923
Ids sort by time, are unique as long as machine ids are unique, and fit B-tree indexes better than random UUIDs.

12
00:00:54.203 --> 00:00:59.537
If the clock goes backwards, refuse to generate (or wait) rather than risk duplicates.

13
00:01:00.887 --> 00:01:02.231
Here is the diagram.

