114
00:09:32.828 --> 00:09:33.703
Part 8.

115
00:09:33.983 --> 00:09:35.092
Common mistakes.

116
00:09:36.342 --> 00:09:39.158
Duplicate machine ids after auto-scaling.

117
00:09:39.438 --> 00:09:45.027
Assign them from ZooKeeper or etcd, a DB lease, or the pod's ordinal in a StatefulSet.

118
00:09:45.307 --> 00:09:48.934
Ignoring clock-backwards events, which produce silent duplicates.

119
00:09:49.214 --> 00:09:52.371
Assuming ids are strictly time-ordered across machines.

120
00:09:52.651 --> 00:09:57.601
They're only roughly ordered (clock skew), so don't use them as an event-ordering guarantee.

121
00:09:57.881 --> 00:10:01.465
Exposing them publicly when the business volume is sensitive.

122
00:10:01.745 --> 00:10:04.817
Ids reveal creation time and give a rough rate.

123
00:10:06.167 --> 00:10:07.063
Part 9.

124
00:10:07.343 --> 00:10:08.687
Follow-up questions.

125
00:10:09.937 --> 00:10:10.811
Question.

126
00:10:11.091 --> 00:10:16.126
Snowflake versus UUIDv4 versus UUIDv7 versus DB sequences?

127
00:10:16.406 --> 00:10:17.771
Thirty-second answer:

128
00:10:18.051 --> 00:10:25.091
UUIDv4: no coordination, but random, so it fragments B-tree indexes, and it's 128-bit.

129
00:10:25.371 --> 00:10:30.641
UUIDv7: time-ordered 128-bit, standardised, a good default today.

130
00:10:30.921 --> 00:10:34.825
DB sequences: simple, but a bottleneck across regions.

131
00:10:35.105 --> 00:10:40.395
Snowflake: compact 64-bit and time-ordered, but needs machine-id management.

132
00:10:41.745 --> 00:10:42.620
Question.

133
00:10:42.900 --> 00:10:46.868
How do you get more than 4,096 ids per ms per node?

134
00:10:47.148 --> 00:10:48.513
Thirty-second answer:

135
00:10:48.793 --> 00:10:58.457
Reallocate bits (fewer machine bits, more sequence bits), run more generators per node with distinct machine ids, or batch-allocate ids to callers.

136
